          "title": "Happiness and Prosperity in Family & Living Levels",
          "markdown": "## 2. Happiness and Prosperity in Family & Living Levels\n\n> **Exam Definition:** At the level of daily living, **Happiness** arises continuously when Right Understanding governs inner desires, thoughts, and expectations and expresses itself as **Trust and Respect** in relationships; **Prosperity** arises in the family when Body needs are identified cleanly and surplus physical facility is produced ethically without exploiting others or nature.\n\nLet us revisit **Happiness and Prosperity** directly through the lens of **Unit 2** (*how they unfold in a real student household*). Notice how common family tension arises around food, gadgets, and career anxiety. Because parents want their children to be \"happy and prosperous,\" they urge them tirelessly toward big salaries—yet confuse *money* (**Physical Facility**) with *Prosperity* and *Happiness*. In truth, no house lacks Happiness because it only has one TV instead of four; a house loses Happiness when family members shout, compare cousins unfairly, or hide problems out of fear!\n\nTwo practical tests confirm whether **Prosperity** truly exists inside a family or student life: (1) Does every member know clearly how much food, clothing, electronics, and living space keeps the **Body healthy and comfortable** (*finite limit*)? (2) Does the household **produce/earn comfortably more** than that finite requirement *without cheating humans or exhausting nature*? Notice that **Prosperity naturally inspires Sharing**: someone who genuinely feels *\"I have more than enough\"* never hoards frantically or envies a neighbor's new car—they gladly support a struggling cousin's tuition or nourish community spaces!\n\n* **Simple Student Example:** Take two college project partners. Partner A shares useful online study videos and notes freely (`feeling prosperous in knowledge and secure in oneself`), boosting both friends' scores and creating warm camaraderie (**Mutual Happiness + Prosperity**). Partner B hides helpful coding resources hoping peers fail exams (`sense of scarcity/deprivation`), staying anxious every minute lest someone overtake them.\n* **How to Apply in Life:** Check this week whether you hold a feeling of *abundance* or *scarcity* toward your classmates—sharing class notes transparently builds deep mutual trust and proves inner Prosperity in practice!"
